earth's oceans are home  to a symphony of aquatic life  from gargantuan whales to tiny shrimp  and coral habitats mixed in with the  calls of creatures is the music of earth  itself  from earthquakes to the patter of rain  on the surface  a new study assessed how these sonic  sources have  fared and often faltered in response to  another  more recent phenomenon  the sounds of human civilization  compared to other human threats to  marine ecosystems  such as oil spills trash and overfishing  sound pollution has taken a back seat in  regulatory debates  many scientists from oceanographers to  marine biologists to ecologists  have uncovered the dangers that human  noise poses to the world's oceans  but to date their observations haven't  led to concrete policy efforts  the new review in science dredged up  data on ocean life  and sound collected from isolated  disciplines  hoping to quantify the impacts of human  activity  on ocean soundscapes the authors divided  ocean sounds into three sources  biological geological and human  or biophony geophony and anthropone  under biophony fell an array of sounds  produced by sea animals  the crackling of crustaceans foraging  for food  the trills of seals as they breed in the  arctic during spring  the clicks and whistles of dolphins  communicating with their pod  geophony consisted of mother nature's  ceaseless songs  hurricanes deep sea faults and polar ice  cracking  at the crux of the research was  anthropone the drumbeat of  industrialized society  global shipping is a huge contributor  it's increased its low frequency ocean  noise by 32 times  offshore pile driving has a large  chronic impact  and fishing by removing animals core to  ecosystems  has quieted habitats the authors found  clear  evidence that the barrage of human-made  sound affects ocean life  438 papers showed human sounds have a  negative  impact on marine animals the evidence  was strongest for mammals  fish and invertebrates  [Music]  researchers also fear climate change has  harmful effects on ocean soundscapes  the great barrier reef has declined  significantly in recent decades  becoming far quieter coral reefs are  known to play  key roles in species settling  reproducing and hunting  animals that depend on reese's habitat  noise to breed and forage  could struggle to adapt to such drastic  change  the authors see reason for hope that  mitigation efforts can make a difference  a recent study conducted near the port  of vancouver  where shipping traffic decreased by 21  during the kovit 19 pandemic showed an  average  1.5 decibel decrease in nearby waterways  and changes to ship machinery have  proven effective  at increasing fuel efficiency while  reducing noise  the researchers are amassing a library  of ocean sounds  which they plan to analyze and diagnose  with the help of machine learning  coupling this with the reviews findings  the researchers believe they can demand  the attention of policymakers  and bring ocean's soundscapes into the  global spotlight  you
 

